---
import Leaderboard from '../components/Leaderboard.astro';
import SubscribePanel from '../components/SubscribePanel.astro';
import { analysis } from '../config/site';
import { loadRepositoryViews } from '../lib/snapshot';
import '../styles/site.css';

const windowDays = analysis.windowDays;
const views = await loadRepositoryViews();
const builtAt = new Date();
const builtStamp = `${builtAt.toISOString().replace('T', ' ').slice(0, 16)} UTC`;
const icon = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/favicon.svg`.replace(/\/{2,}/g, '/');
// Read by CI: publishing a page where every figure says "No data" would be a
// silent failure, so the build refuses rather than shipping an empty board.
const hasMetrics = views.some((view) => view.snapshot.discussions.length > 0);
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer" />
    <link rel="icon" href={icon} type="image/svg+xml" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="GitHub Discussions engagement metrics and Atom subscriptions for CNCF projects."
    />
    <title>Discussion engagement</title>
  </head>
  <body data-has-metrics={hasMetrics ? 'true' : 'false'}>
    <main>
      <header class="page-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Prototype</p>
        <h1>Discussion engagement</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          How busy a project's discussion board is, who is taking part, and how quickly
          questions get answered, over the last {windowDays} days. Subscribe to follow a
          board without checking this page.
        </p>
        <p class="built">
          <span>Measured when this page was built, </span>
          <time datetime={builtAt.toISOString()}>{builtStamp}</time><span>.</span>
        </p>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="leaderboard-heading">
        <div class="section-heading">
          <h2 id="leaderboard-heading">Leaderboard</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            One row per enrolled repository. Ranking, filtering, and org-wide totals
            arrive with the multi-repository pass.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Leaderboard views={views} />
      </section>

      {views.map((view) => <SubscribePanel view={view} />)}

      <section class="methodology" aria-labelledby="methodology-heading">
        <h2 id="methodology-heading">How these numbers are produced</h2>

        <p>
          The discussion board is read through the GitHub GraphQL API while this page is
          built, using the workflow's own credentials. Nothing is asked of you, and no
          token is shipped to your browser. The figures are therefore current as of the
          build time shown above, not as of your visit. The Atom feeds are live.
        </p>

        <h3>Computed from a single read</h3>
        <p>
          Every metric here, including the sparkline and the trend, comes from timestamps
          the API returns alongside each discussion, comment, and reply. Because each
          contribution carries its own <code>createdAt</code>, one read reconstructs when
          activity happened and compares one period against another. No stored snapshot
          history is required.
        </p>

        <h3>What would need stored history</h3>
        <p>
          Metrics about state that GitHub only reports as it currently stands. Whether a
          question is answered is a present-tense flag; <code>answerChosenAt</code>
          recovers when an answer was accepted, but nothing recovers a question that was
          answered and then unaccepted. Deleted discussions and comments are absent
          entirely, and a discussion reports only the category it sits in today. This
          prototype keeps no snapshots, so none of that is reconstructed.
        </p>

        <h3>Definitions</h3>
        <dl class="definitions">
          <dt>Window</dt>
          <dd>The {windowDays} days ending when the page was built.</dd>

          <dt>Participants</dt>
          <dd>
            Distinct accounts that opened a discussion, commented, or replied within the
            window. Bots count. Deleted accounts cannot be attributed and are excluded.
          </dd>

          <dt>Comments</dt>
          <dd>
            Comments and threaded replies created within the window, including those on
            discussions opened long before it.
          </dd>

          <dt>Answered</dt>
          <dd>
            The share of discussions opened in the window, in categories that accept
            answers, that currently have an accepted answer. Categories that do not accept
            answers are excluded rather than counted as unanswered.
          </dd>

          <dt>First response</dt>
          <dd>
            Median time between a discussion opening and the first contribution from
            somebody other than its author. Discussions with no such response are not
            counted, so this measures the speed of answers given, not the odds of getting
            one.
          </dd>

          <dt>New and returning</dt>
          <dd>
            A participant is new when the window holds their first contribution to this
            board. This needs the whole board to be read; if the read stops early, the
            metric reports insufficient history rather than guessing.
          </dd>

          <dt>Trend</dt>
          <dd>
            Total contributions in the window against the immediately preceding window of
            equal length.
          </dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>When a number is missing</h3>
        <p>
          A cell without a number never means zero. It says which of three things
          happened. <span class="state-word">No activity</span> means the window was read
          successfully and nothing happened in it.
          <span class="state-word">Insufficient history</span> means the board was read,
          but not far enough back to answer that particular question, usually because the
          request budget or the rate limit ran out.
          <span class="state-word">No data</span> means the read failed, so the metric was
          never measured. A failure affecting one metric leaves the others, and every
          subscription link, intact.
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer class="page-footer">
        <p>
          Enrolled repositories come from a single configuration file. Sourcing that list
          from the CNCF Landscape is a data change, not a code change.
        </p>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script>
      import '../scripts/dashboard';
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
